WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:05.710
Infinitech's Regulated Data Platform is a
reference implementation for teams that

2
00:00:05.710 --> 00:00:10.680
move sensitive records through AWS.
Records arrive through Firehose, land in

3
00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:15.650
an encrypted lake, and load into Redshift
Serverless, with backups copied to a

4
00:00:15.650 --> 00:00:19.640
second region. Everything in this
walkthrough was recorded live, on two

5
00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:20.680
demo cycles.

6
00:00:23.034 --> 00:00:28.514
The platform is Terraform, applied in
two steps. A prepare run plans the core

7
00:00:28.874 --> 00:00:33.264
stack, here one hundred thirty-four
resources, and runs the policy gate

8
00:00:33.264 --> 00:00:38.434
against that plan. It saves the plan
with checksums. A person reads the saved

9
00:00:38.434 --> 00:00:42.254
plan before anything changes.
The execute run then checks those

10
00:00:42.614 --> 00:00:46.914
checksums and applies that exact
plan, so what was reviewed is what gets

11
00:00:47.274 --> 00:00:47.594
built.

12
00:00:49.956 --> 00:00:54.116
To show the gate stopping a bad change,
we opened a pull request that makes the

13
00:00:54.116 --> 00:00:58.986
Redshift workgroup publicly accessible.
Terraform plans the change.

14
00:00:59.076 --> 00:01:03.556
The policy gate rejects it: its
no-public-resources rule names the

15
00:01:03.556 --> 00:01:08.276
workgroup and fails the check, so the
change cannot merge. The same gate

16
00:01:08.276 --> 00:01:13.496
requires customer-managed keys, data
classification tags, and approved regions,

17
00:01:13.576 --> 00:01:16.856
and it runs on pull requests and before
every apply.

18
00:01:19.238 --> 00:01:23.148
Records arrive through Amazon
Data Firehose. This load sent fifty

19
00:01:23.148 --> 00:01:28.638
thousand generated test events, five
hundred of them malformed on purpose.

20
00:01:28.678 --> 00:01:32.488
Firehose buffers them for up to sixty
seconds, then writes them to the

21
00:01:32.488 --> 00:01:36.258
landing bucket. There are no servers to
run, and the stream's error log

22
00:01:36.258 --> 00:01:37.118
stayed empty.

23
00:01:39.470 --> 00:01:42.880
The lake has three zones,
each its own bucket.

24
00:01:42.950 --> 00:01:46.480
Landing keeps what arrived,
exactly as it arrived.

25
00:01:46.550 --> 00:01:50.400
Curated holds the validated
records, beside the manifest each

26
00:01:50.660 --> 00:01:55.520
Redshift load used. Quarantine keeps the
records that failed validation for review:

27
00:01:55.630 --> 00:01:58.620
here, the five hundred bad
events from each batch.

28
00:01:58.930 --> 00:02:02.560
Every bucket is versioned and
encrypted with a customer-managed key.

29
00:02:05.024 --> 00:02:09.424
Every ten minutes, a Step Functions state
machine looks for new data.

30
00:02:09.424 --> 00:02:14.464
Each step is a short-lived Fargate task.
Curate validates the records and

31
00:02:14.464 --> 00:02:19.024
splits them. Load copies them into
Redshift and merges them, so a replayed

32
00:02:19.024 --> 00:02:23.624
file adds no rows.
Verify checks the tables: one row per

33
00:02:23.624 --> 00:02:28.754
event, and no extra rows from a replay.
This run took about four minutes, and all

34
00:02:28.824 --> 00:02:32.334
seven checks passed on forty-nine thousand
five hundred events.

35
00:02:34.846 --> 00:02:40.036
The warehouse is Redshift Serverless at
four RPUs, and its compute is billed only

36
00:02:40.036 --> 00:02:43.956
while it runs queries.
It sits in private subnets with public

37
00:02:44.136 --> 00:02:48.196
access turned off. The policy gate checks
those settings on every plan.

38
00:02:50.830 --> 00:02:55.550
People who need to see the data get
a read-only role. This presenter signs in

39
00:02:55.550 --> 00:02:59.900
to Query editor through single sign-on,
with no database password.

40
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:04.700
The role can read the analytics tables:
the three event types add up to all

41
00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:09.330
forty-nine thousand five hundred events.
When the same user tries to delete them,

42
00:03:09.330 --> 00:03:12.110
Redshift refuses with permission denied.

43
00:03:14.542 --> 00:03:19.652
A delete has to reach every copy of
a record. Here a delete request for one

44
00:03:19.652 --> 00:03:24.742
synthetic subject removed its row from the
warehouse, and the erase run then cleaned

45
00:03:24.802 --> 00:03:31.412
both lakes. In us-east-1, the eraser
rewrote two files and deleted two old

46
00:03:31.582 --> 00:03:36.392
versions, and its check found the subject
in none of the three versions it read.

47
00:03:36.422 --> 00:03:42.042
The standby's lake in us-west-2 has its
own eraser. It deleted one version, and

48
00:03:42.082 --> 00:03:46.072
its check came back clean.
In the standby's bucket, that file now has

49
00:03:46.072 --> 00:03:48.912
a single version, with no older copy left.

50
00:03:51.282 --> 00:03:54.722
The drill stopped the Curate task three
times in a row.

51
00:03:54.802 --> 00:03:58.762
Step Functions retried it twice, then
marked the run failed.

52
00:03:58.842 --> 00:04:02.872
The run was then redriven from the failed
step, without starting over, and it

53
00:04:03.112 --> 00:04:07.502
finished green. Start to finish took six
minutes and forty-two seconds, and the

54
00:04:07.502 --> 00:04:09.982
execution's event log keeps every attempt.

55
00:04:12.362 --> 00:04:16.742
Two CloudWatch dashboards cover health and
operations: how runs ended,

56
00:04:17.042 --> 00:04:22.062
how fresh the ingested data is, ingest
volume, and warehouse capacity.

57
00:04:22.242 --> 00:04:26.682
The forced failure shows as a spike.
The run-failed alarm fired less than a

58
00:04:26.682 --> 00:04:30.132
minute later, and the
alarm for no successful run stayed quiet,

59
00:04:30.132 --> 00:04:31.952
because the redrive succeeded.

60
00:04:34.474 --> 00:04:39.814
Every pipeline alarm has an alarm action
and an OK action, and a description with

61
00:04:39.814 --> 00:04:44.584
its severity, its impact, a runbook link
and a dashboard. To test the

62
00:04:45.054 --> 00:04:47.894
Firehose alarm, we blocked
Firehose from writing to the

63
00:04:47.894 --> 00:04:52.814
landing bucket. Once a record had waited
more than thirty minutes, the alarm fired.

64
00:04:52.874 --> 00:04:56.634
The email arrived in the same second, and
PagerDuty opened an incident

65
00:04:57.114 --> 00:05:00.384
one second later, with the
same description. Someone on call

66
00:05:00.384 --> 00:05:04.114
acknowledged it. After the block was
lifted and the held records were

67
00:05:04.114 --> 00:05:09.264
delivered, CloudWatch sent OK, and the
incident resolved itself. Pages go to the

68
00:05:09.354 --> 00:05:13.694
customer's own on-call, through any tool
that subscribes to the alarm topic.

69
00:05:16.158 --> 00:05:20.228
For disaster recovery, snapshots of the
warehouse are copied from

70
00:05:20.888 --> 00:05:26.068
us-east-1 to us-west-2. This copy arrived
about nineteen minutes after the snapshot

71
00:05:26.068 --> 00:05:31.038
was taken. A warm standby workgroup
waits in us-west-2 as well, kept loaded

72
00:05:31.108 --> 00:05:33.488
from the curated files that
replicate there.

73
00:05:35.810 --> 00:05:39.690
Then we failed over.
The drill stopped the primary's pipeline

74
00:05:39.690 --> 00:05:43.080
and timed two paths.
The default restores the copied

75
00:05:43.550 --> 00:05:46.910
snapshot: the warehouse answered queries
again eight point two minutes

76
00:05:47.250 --> 00:05:50.800
after the failure, and its
twenty point eight minute data-loss window

77
00:05:50.870 --> 00:05:54.150
is the age of the snapshot the
drill restored. The warm standby

78
00:05:54.670 --> 00:05:58.010
option held all ninety-nine thousand
events two point one minutes

79
00:05:58.570 --> 00:06:00.760
after the failure, with no data lost.

80
00:06:03.272 --> 00:06:08.452
Every demo cycle ends by tearing it all
down: two hundred ninety-four resources

81
00:06:08.452 --> 00:06:12.692
across three stacks, each destroy plan
read before it ran.

82
00:06:12.692 --> 00:06:17.192
A zero-resource report then checks
both regions. It found nothing billable

83
00:06:17.192 --> 00:06:18.292
left from the demo.

84
00:06:20.702 --> 00:06:24.832
Infinitech stands the platform up in your
AWS accounts and hands it over,

85
00:06:25.322 --> 00:06:29.622
with the repository, the runbook, and the
evidence from your own drills.

86
00:06:29.662 --> 00:06:33.862
Ready for GovCloud. To plan a deployment,
contact Infinitech.
